    "summary": "Dark Star/Cumberland blaze, Bird Song jazzy, crisp SBD capture",
    "blurb": "The show features a soaring Dark Star that segues into a fiery Cumberland Blues, plus standout Bird Song and jazzy China Cat > Rider jams. Jerry's guitar is blistering throughout while Keith adds jazz‑savvy textures; Phil provides a solid bass foundation. The soundboard recording is crisp with only minor vocal slip‑ups.",
    "review": "On September 27 1972 at the Stanley Theatre, the Dead delivered one of their quintessential early‑’70s outings. The first set opened with an emotive “Morning Dew” and built momentum through classics like “Friend Of The Devil,” “Black Throated Wind,” and a tight “Playing In The Band.” The second set is where the night truly shines: a luminous “Dark Star” erupts into a high‑octane “Cumberland Blues,” followed by the jazzy, soaring “Bird Song” and the ever‑delightful “China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider” sequence. Keith’s piano adds a nuanced, jazz‑savvy edge to the improvisations while Phil locks in a buttery bass groove, and Jerry’s lead work on Dark Star is nothing short of blistering, even if a vocal slip shows up during “Casey Jones.” The Vernon soundboard capture is remarkably clear – fine‑crisp throughout with no major dropouts – making this performance a must‑listen for anyone hunting that classic 1972 energy.\n\nFans will appreciate the balance of tight songcraft and adventurous jamming, highlighted by the flawless transition from Dark Star into Cumberland Blues and the melodic wanderings in Bird Song. Though a few minor vocal hiccups surface, they hardly detract from an otherwise top‑notch set that captures the band in peak form. This recording stands out as one of the best documented snapshots of the Dead’s early‑’70s magic.",
